# Frontend Standards

## Before Creating Anything

Before creating any new component, page, hook or utility:

1. Search the codebase for an existing implementation that solves the same problem.
2. If one exists, reuse or extend it — do not create a parallel solution.
3. Only create something new when nothing existing can reasonably cover the need.

## Components

- Prefer reusable components over duplicated code.
- Keep components small and focused — one responsibility per component.
- Split large components into smaller, composable pieces.
- Never use inline styles — use CSS modules or a utility-first approach consistently.
- Extend via props or composition before creating a new variant.
- Never build two components that render the same UI pattern differently.

## Separation of Concerns

- UI components must not contain data-fetching or business logic.
- Extract reusable logic into custom hooks (`src/hooks/`).
- Keep data transformation and formatting outside of JSX.
- If a hook already encapsulates a piece of logic, reuse it — do not extract the same logic again elsewhere.

## Data

- Components receive data via props or hooks. They do not fetch and they do not read Markdown files.
- No hardcoded values that belong to the API (labels, lists, amounts, categories, etc.).

## File Organization

```
src/
  components/    # Reusable UI components
  pages/         # Page-level components (one per route)
  hooks/         # Custom React hooks
  services/      # API client used by hooks
  types/         # Shared TypeScript types and interfaces
  utils/         # Pure utility functions
```

## Architectural Consistency

- New screens must follow the same structure as existing pages.
- New features must follow the same data flow pattern already established in the project.
- When in doubt, match what already exists before proposing a new approach.

## Avoid

- Large components that mix layout, logic and data.
- Repeated logic — extract to hooks or utils before duplicating.
- Inline styles (`style={{ ... }}`).
- Hardcoded values that belong to the data layer.
